===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[handle]] [[light]]ly; said with reference to awkward playing on a [[fiddle]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881| title=Tufts of Heather - Volume 1 |author=Edwin Waugh | page=21| passage=“Neaw, owd lad,” said he, as he screwed, first one peg, then another, and '''tweedled''' over little fits of wailing prelude, to get the tones he wanted.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1905| title=The Diary of William Bentley D.D., |author=William Bentley | page=267| passage=Hark, —his tortured catgut squals He tickles every string, to every note He bends his pliant neck.—The fond yielding Maid Is '''tweedled''' into Love.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete|by extension}} To influence as if by fiddling; to [[coax]]; to [[allure]].
#: {{synonyms|en|wheedle}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Freeholder|issue=3|date=December 30, 1716|passage=A fiddler brought in with him a body of lusty young fellows, whom he had '''tweedled''' into the service.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1897|work=The Vassar Miscellany |title=When Vincent Played the Title Rôle|author=M. Gerturde Fulton|volume=27|page=141|passage=But I '''tweedled''' him into letting you try, and here you are, a real member of the company. }}
# To [[twiddle]]. <!--proscribed?-->
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1842| title=Ten Thousand A-year - Volume 1 |author=Samuel Warren | page=59| passage=As it was, he '''tweedled''' the letter about in his hands for about five minutes, in a musing mood, and then stepped with it into Mr. Gammon's room. }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 16, 1845|work=Littell's Living Age |title=Mrs. Mark Luke; or, West Country Exclusives|author=|volume=6|page=318|passage=Bob Pirgivie now '''tweedled''' his thumbs in double quick time, and rapidly sent around queer horizontal glances under his shaggy brows.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007| title=Rise of the Weaponsmaster |author=Bryan Butikofer | page=168| passage=He blinked slowly and '''tweedled''' his long brown mustache between his fingers. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008| title=The Uses of Enchantment |author=Heidi Julavits | page=51| passage=Mary noticed Gaby absently '''tweedling''' the green ribbon hanging from her coat's buttonhole.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=Vor|author=James Blish | page=| passage=John Hammerkein, the bulky, slow-oiced communications officer, '''tweedled''' knobs in the far corner, under the bulletin board where the field's traffic pattern was posted.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021| title=The New Deal and Texas History|author=Ronald E. Goodwin | page=63| passage=he (Driver) leaned back in his easy chair and playfully '''tweedled''' the ears of his devoted dog, who was curled up cat-like fashion in his master's lap.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang}} To sell fake jewellery as genuine.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1925| title=Twenty-five Years in Six Prisons |author=Eustace Jervis | page=17| passage=I am afraid that the knowing author of the “cracking a-crib” book would be flummoxed by '''tweedling'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Duffy|author=Dan Kavanagh | page=| passage=Girls, smokes, bit of smack, mossing, '''tweedling'''; a very democratic villain, Mr Salvatore.}}
# To make a [[shrill]] or [[trill]]ing sound
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861| title=The Autobiography and Correspondence of Mary Granville|author=Mary Granville Delany | page=182| passage=Yesterday I dined at the Percivals, and '''tweedled''' away upon a lovely harpsichord, and I was not bid to “mind my time.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002| title=Cat in a Midnight Choir|author=Carole Nelson Douglas | page=90| passage=Something '''tweedled''', and Temple jumped. Every new car had its own literal bells and whistles that told you to take the key out of the ignition, or put your seat belt on, or to turn off your headlights.}}
#*{{quote-book|en|2010|Carolyn J. Rose; Mike Nettleton|Sometimes a Great Commotion|passage=From deep inside my purse, my cell phone '''tweedled''' the opening bars of “{{w|Start Me Up}}.”}}
# To say in a high-pitched voice.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=Headlong|author=Michael Frayn | page=| passage=Hurt that the Churts and I were exchanging fatuities over yet another second-rate painting instead of '''tweedling''' appreciatively over the extraordinary and beautiful child she was rocking on the other side of the room?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=The Tale of Mark Levine |author=Michael D. Lieberman | page=184| passage="Oh, ''bien sur''. Monsieur Raphael has only the ''kindest'' things to say," Azter '''tweedled'''. “Always.”}}
# To [[trifle]] or [[play]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1941| title=Hearings|author=United States. Congress. House. Committee on the Judiciary | page=248| passage=That exemption was not intended to be a hair splitting elusive hope to be '''tweedled''' away by administrative interpretation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008| title=Prescription for Survival|author=Bernard Lown | page=| passage=Intellectual '''tweedling''' is not within my character.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=About Hare: The Playwright and the Work|author=Richard Boon | page=| passage=There aren't many things that make playwriting easy, but the fluency of the English language is a tremendous help. Now if you choose to write in Ur Chinese, you haven't got that, you've only got the meaning of what is being said. And that was bracing, after years of '''tweedling''' round with words.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021| title=Donald Barthelme: Collected Stories |author=Donald Barthelme; Charles McGrath | page=| passage=The organization is not to be '''tweedled''' with.}}
# To [[go]]; to [[proceed]] without much [[enthusiasm]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907| title=Report of Proceedings of the Annual Session |author=Montana Horticultural Society | page=24| passage=You all have undoubtedly noticed men in the rural pursuits '''tweedling''' along through life and finally end it all identical with what it was when they began: no better, no worse.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=Trizophrenia: Inside the Minds of a Triathlete|author=Jef Mallett | page=| passage=moving slowly but moving nonetheless; and close enough that if I quit and he kept '''tweedling''' along, he would finish with more miles than I and I'd be even more depressed long after I stopped shivering.  He did keep '''tweedling''' along, but so did I.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013| title=Saratoga Strongbox|author=Stephen Dobyns | page=| passage=What was discretion but chickenheartedness and a capitulation to age? If I '''tweedled''' back to Saratoga without doing anything, then I might as well kiss Rosemary good-bye.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| title=The Oxford Handbook of Charles Brockden Brown |author=Philip Barnard; Hilary Emmett; Stephen Shapiro | page=85| passage=In the first part of the scene, the Frenchman dominates the monkey while '''tweedling''' along blithely as his Saint-Dominguan servants look with confusion on what surrounds them. }}
# {{lb|en|of two people}} To [[move]] or [[speak]] in unison (like {{w|Tweedledum and Tweedledee}})
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1919| title=Tripping With the Tucker Twins|author=Nell Speed | page=| passage="I shall feel myself a failure as a chaperone surely," remarked Mrs.Green. "We think you a tremendous success," '''tweedled''' the twins.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1934 | title=Frankie in Wonderland|author=Latham R. Reed | page=17| passage=“Why, they're '''tweedling''' now, " said little Frankie and sure enough they were, and '''tweedling''' in perfect unison too, without any expression whatsoever.“}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021| title=The Carter Girls of Carter House|author=Emma Speed Sampson | page=| passage="{{...}} cold storage,” soothed Page Allison. “Not a chance,” '''tweedled''' the twins.}}

